# Data Interpretation: Tables and Charts

> A data interpretation (DI) set gives you a table, bar graph, line graph or pie
> chart and asks four to six questions on it. SSC CGL Tier 1 and 2, CHSL and RRB
> NTPC papers all carry DI, and the questions repeat a small group of
> calculations: percent change, average, ratio, share of total and angle. This
> lesson shows how to read a table in text form, the five core calculations, and
> the traps that cost marks.

## 1. Read Before You Calculate

[definition]
**Data interpretation** is the skill of extracting numbers from a table or chart
and answering questions on them using averages, ratios, percentages and simple
comparison.
[/definition]

Before any arithmetic, check four things.

| Check | Why it matters |
| --- | --- |
| Title | tells you what is measured |
| Units | lakh, thousand, percent, tonnes or rupees change the answer |
| Row and column labels | prevents reading the wrong year or category |
| What the question asks | total, average, ratio, percent or difference |

A common slip is mixing units in a single answer, such as comparing a figure in
thousands with one in lakhs. Convert first, then calculate.

## 2. A Worked Table

Student enrolment in three colleges (original data for practice):

| College | 2022 | 2023 | 2024 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| A | 420 | 450 | 480 |
| B | 300 | 330 | 297 |
| C | 250 | 275 | 330 |
| Total | 970 | 1055 | 1107 |

Totals are computed column by column: for 2022, $420 + 300 + 250 = 970$.
Check the grand total two ways (row sums and column sums) to catch mistakes.

**Quick questions on this table.**
- Total for College A: $420 + 450 + 480 = 1350$.
- Average yearly enrolment of A: $\frac{1350}{3} = 450$.
- B in 2024 versus 2022: it rose from 300 to 297 after peaking, a net fall of 3.

## 3. The Five Core Calculations

@math label="eq:percent-change"
\text{Percent change} = \frac{\text{new} - \text{old}}{\text{old}} \times 100
@/math

| Calculation | Formula | Example from the table |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Average | $\frac{\text{sum}}{\text{count}}$ | A: $\frac{1350}{3} = 450$ |
| Ratio | first : second, simplified | A to B in 2023: $450 : 330 = 15 : 11$ |
| Percent change | divide by the **old** value | B, 2022 to 2023: $\frac{30}{300} = 10\%$ |
| Share of total | $\frac{\text{part}}{\text{total}} \times 100$ | A in 2023: $\frac{450}{1055} \approx 42.7\%$ |
| Difference | larger minus smaller | C minus B in 2024: $330 - 297 = 33$ |

[important]
Percent change always divides by the **old** (base) value. B fell from 330 to
297 in 2024, a drop of $\frac{33}{330} = 10\%$. C rose 20% in 2024 on a base of
275. Dividing by the new value is the most common error.
[/important]

## 4. Bar Graphs and Line Graphs in Text Form

A bar graph or line graph is a table in disguise. In the exam, read each value
off the axis, write the numbers down as a mini-table, then calculate.

| Feature | What it tells you |
| --- | --- |
| Height of a bar | the value for that category |
| Slope of a line | rate of rise or fall between two points |
| Gap between two lines | the difference between two series |
| Axis start | check whether it starts at zero |

Tips for line graphs: a steeper segment means a bigger change, but percent
change depends on the base, so compute it rather than judging by eye. Always
look for the year with the **highest** growth, which is not always the year
with the highest value.

**Percentage points versus percent.** If a pass rate rises from 60% to 72%, it
rose by 12 **percentage points**, but the **percent increase** is
$\frac{12}{60} \times 100 = 20\%$. The two are different answers.

## 5. Pie Charts and Degrees

A pie chart divides a whole into sectors. The full circle is $360^\circ$, so
$1\% = 3.6^\circ$.

| Item | Share | Angle |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Rent | 30% | $108^\circ$ |
| Food | 25% | $90^\circ$ |
| Transport | 15% | $54^\circ$ |
| Savings | 20% | $72^\circ$ |
| Other | 10% | $36^\circ$ |
| Total | 100% | $360^\circ$ |

Convert both ways. From angle to value: $\frac{\text{angle}}{360} \times \text{total}$.
For a total monthly income of Rs 45,000, Savings (20%) equals Rs 9,000.

| Question type | Method |
| --- | --- |
| Value of a sector | share $\times$ total |
| Difference of two sectors | difference of percentages $\times$ total |
| Angle for a given share | share $\times 3.6$ |
| Share from an angle | angle $\div 3.6$ |

## 6. Speed Tricks and Exam Traps

- **Approximate smartly**: when options are far apart, round the numbers (e.g.
  1055 as 1050) and choose the nearest option.
- **Compare by cross-multiplying** instead of computing two decimals.
- **Use the unit method**: if the unit is in thousands, drop three zeros through
  the whole calculation and restore it at the end.
- **Missing value**: if the average of $n$ items is given, total $= n \times$
  average, so the missing item is total minus the known sum.

| Trap | Fix |
| --- | --- |
| Dividing by the new value | Divide by the old value |
| Confusing percent and percentage points | Compute both and read the question |
| Wrong year or row | Underline the year in the question |
| Mixed units | Convert before calculating |
| Highest value versus highest growth | Calculate growth separately |

[example]
Using the enrolment table, which college grew fastest in 2024? A rose
$\frac{30}{450} = 6.67\%$, B fell 10% and C rose $\frac{55}{275} = 20\%$.
College C grew fastest. Note that A's rise of 30 is smaller than C's 55 and also
smaller in percent, so always compute the percentage rather than judging by
the size of the numbers.
[/example]

[summary]
Read the title, units and labels first, then pick the calculation the question
needs. Average is sum over count; ratio compares two values in simplest form;
percent change divides by the old value; share of total is part over total times
100; and a pie chart converts at 3.6 degrees per percent. Remember the
difference between percent and percentage points, calculate growth rather than
judging it by eye, and double-check units. Round smartly when options are far
apart and write chart values as a small table before computing.
[/summary]
